# Proof

We determine the product of an empty family of similar algebras
$\prod\langle \mathbf{A}_i \mid i \in I \rangle$ with $I = \emptyset$.

## Universal property of the product

Recall that the product $\prod_{i \in I} \mathbf{A}_i$ of a family of similar algebras (all of the same type/signature $\mathcal{F}$) is an algebra $\mathbf{P}$ together with homomorphisms $\pi_i : \mathbf{P} \to \mathbf{A}_i$ for each $i \in I$, satisfying the universal property:

> For every algebra $\mathbf{B}$ (of the same type) and every family of homomorphisms $f_i : \mathbf{B} \to \mathbf{A}_i$ ($i \in I$), there exists a **unique** homomorphism $f : \mathbf{B} \to \mathbf{P}$ such that $\pi_i \circ f = f_i$ for all $i \in I$.

## Specializing to $I = \emptyset$

When $I = \emptyset$:
- There are **no** projection maps $\pi_i$ to specify.
- The indexing family of homomorphisms $f_i : \mathbf{B} \to \mathbf{A}_i$ is **empty**, so the compatibility conditions $\pi_i \circ f = f_i$ are vacuous.

Thus the universal property reduces to:

> For every algebra $\mathbf{B}$ of the given type, there exists a **unique** homomorphism $f : \mathbf{B} \to \mathbf{P}$.

This is precisely the defining property of a **terminal object** in the category of algebras of type $\mathcal{F}$.

## The terminal object is the one-element (trivial) algebra

Let $\mathbf{1}$ denote the algebra whose underlying set is a singleton $\{e\}$, and in which every $n$-ary operation symbol $g \in \mathcal{F}$ is interpreted as the unique map $g^{\mathbf{1}} : \{e\}^n \to \{e\}$, i.e., $g^{\mathbf{1}}(e, \dots, e) = e$.

**Claim:** $\mathbf{1}$ is the terminal object.

*Proof of claim.* For any algebra $\mathbf{B}$ of type $\mathcal{F}$ with underlying set $B$, there is exactly one set-theoretic function $B \to \{e\}$, namely the constant map $f(b) = e$. We verify it is a homomorphism: for any $n$-ary $g \in \mathcal{F}$ and any $b_1, \dots, b_n \in B$,
$$
f\bigl(g^{\mathbf{B}}(b_1, \dots, b_n)\bigr) = e = g^{\mathbf{1}}(e, \dots, e) = g^{\mathbf{1}}\bigl(f(b_1), \dots, f(b_n)\bigr).
$$
Hence $f$ is a homomorphism, and it is the unique one since it is the unique set map $B \to \{e\}$. $\square$

## Uniqueness up to isomorphism

Terminal objects are unique up to unique isomorphism. Therefore the empty product is uniquely determined (up to isomorphism) as the one-element algebra.

## Conclusion

The product of an empty family of similar algebras is the **one-element (trivial) algebra** $\mathbf{1}$ — the terminal object in the category of algebras of the given type. Concretely, its underlying set is a singleton $\{e\}$, and every operation sends every tuple of $e$'s to $e$.

